<path d="M 280 65 Q 295 50 308 65" stroke="#1317E4" stroke-width="1" fill="none" opacity=".5"/>
</svg>
</div>

<div class="card-story">"Implementation is one-time. Evolution is forever. Only No-Code keeps you free."</div>

<dl class="card-meta">
<dt>Buyer mindset</dt><dd>Owner-operator — long-term cost of dependency.</dd>
<dt>Strength</dt><dd>Renders Velo Working's whole thesis visually. Assumes implementation done.</dd>
<dt>Bonus</dt><dd>Doesn't undermine Velo's implementation revenue — both axes are post-deployment.</dd>
</dl>
</div>

</div>

<!-- RECOMMENDATION BLOCK -->

<aside class="rec">
<div class="rec-eyebrow">Recommendation</div>
<h2>Framing C wins because it shows the <span class="accent">ongoing</span> relationship.</h2>
<p>Framings A and B both place No-Code in the right quadrant — but they describe what's true <strong>before</strong> the system goes live. Once a category is bought and deployed, its cost-to-buy stops mattering. What matters is the rent it charges for every subsequent change.</p>
<p>Framing C is the only one of the three where both axes describe the <strong>life of the system after Velo Working leaves the room</strong>. That's the actual moat — and the only framing that doesn't accidentally reduce Velo's own implementation work to "the cheap option."</p>

<div class="rec-grid">
<div class="rec-cell is-base">
<div class="ax">Framing A · Economics</div>
<div class="label">Strong but limiting</div>
<div class="desc">Frames Jodoo as the budget choice. Good for cost-conscious buyers, weak for premium positioning.</div>
</div>
<div class="rec-cell is-base">
<div class="ax">Framing B · Organisational</div>
<div class="label">Smart but indirect</div>
<div class="desc">Requires buyer to translate "needs IT department" into "we don't have one." Works on reflective buyers, slower on transactional ones.</div>
</div>
<div class="rec-cell">
<div class="ax">Framing C · Battlefield</div>
<div class="label">Both axes favour Jodoo</div>
<div class="desc">No tradeoff narrative for the buyer to entertain. ERP sits in the worst quadrant, Jodoo alone in the best. Mirrors the article's closing line.</div>
</div>
</div>
</aside>

